:   * cite40†Copyright (CC BY) L50:   * cite41†Trademark L51:   * cite42†Privacy Policy L52:   * cite43†Thanks L53: \nL54: cite44†Electronic Frontier Foundation L55: \nL56: cite45†Donate†supporters.eff.org L57: \nL58: cite44†Electronic Frontier Foundation L59:   * cite1†About L60:     * cite2†Contact L61:     * cite3†Press L62:     * cite4†People L63:     * cite5†Opportunities L64:   * cite6†Issues L65:     * cite7†Free Speech L66:     * cite8†Privacy L67:     * cite9†Creativity and Innovation L68:     * cite10†Transparency L69:     * cite11†International L70:     * cite12†Security L71:     * cite13†Artificial Intelligence L72:   * cite14†Our Work L73:     * cite15†Deeplinks Blog L74:     * cite16†Press Releases L75:     * cite17†Events L76:     * cite18†Legal Cases L77:     * cite19†Whitepapers L78:     * cite20†Podcasts L79:     * cite21†Annual Reports L80:   * cite22†Take Action†act.eff.org L81:     * cite22†Action Center†act.eff.org L82:     * cite23†Volunteer L83:     * cite24†Follow EFF L84:   * cite25†Tools L85:     * cite26†Privacy Badger L86:     * cite27†Surveillance Self-Defense L87:     * cite28†Certbot L88:     * cite29†Atlas of Surveillance L89:     * cite30†Cover Your Tracks L90:     * cite31†Street Level Surveillance†sls.eff.org L91:     * cite32†apkeep L92:   * cite33†Shop†shop.eff.org L93:   * cite34†Donate†supporters.eff.org L94:     * cite34†Donate to EFF†supporters.eff.org L95:     * cite33†Shop†shop.eff.org L96:     * cite35†Giving Societies L97:     * cite36†Sponsorships L98:     * cite37†Other Ways to Give L99:     * cite38†Membership FAQ L100: # DraftKings Is Using AI to Supercharge the Harms of Online Behavioral Advertising\nL101: Online sports betting company DraftKings cite46†is using AI to target customers†www.nytimes.com who are most likely to place losing bets and respond to gambling promotions. This kind of targeting is a form of cite47†online behavioral advertising , which is when companies personalize the ads they show you based on the data they’ve collected about you. The more data a company has, the more personalized the ad can be.\nL102: While DraftKings is using AI to supercharge the harmful effects of online behavioral advertising, EFF has long cite48†argued that all behavioral advertising should be banned.\nL103: According to the cite49†New York Times†www.nytimes.com , DraftKings is using its customers’ betting records to train a cite50†machine learning model†hai.stanford.edu to find losing gamblers. Once found, DraftKings sends these customers targeted advertising designed to lure them back to the site to place more bets—bets that DraftKings thinks will be losing ones. DraftKings has a business incentive to keep losing gamblers coming back to their site, because these are the users actually making DraftKings money.\nL104: Unfortunately, those considered “problem gamblers” (people who repeatedly gamble despite harm to themselves, their finances, and their relationships) are highly likely to be targeted by this model. By re-engaging these individuals through targeted promotions aimed at keeping them on the platform, DraftKings is capitalizing on their vulnerability for profit instead of mitigating their risk.\nL105: Predatory online behavioral advertising isn’t new, but companies’ use of AI to process data and target customers has magnified its harms. Online behavioral advertising incentivizes the collection of vast quantities of data to power ad tech. Adding AI into the mix means that even more data is collected to train and refine models.\nL106: Because AI operates as cite51†a black box , the humans building the models can rarely predict which data points are the most useful to the AI, driving them to continuously collect more data. AI also allows companies to process enormous data sets much faster, and, as a result, supercharges the harms of online behavioral advertising.\nL107: A direct consequence of online behavioral advertising is that it provides the data the cite52†surveillance industry needs to run. Data collected for targeted placement of ads is being cite53†sold†calmatters.org to insurance companies, banks, and state and federal government law enforcement agencies cite54†such as CBP .\nL108: ICE is also taking an interest in the data fueling ad tech: earlier this year, ICE published a cite55†Request for Information†sam.gov “seeking information to better understand how the industry’s commercial Big Data and Ad Tech providers can directly support investigations activities.”\nL109: DraftKings seems to be using solely “first party data” to target their ads, meaning that they’re using only the data they collect directly from their users and are not buying any additional data from third parties to fuel their machine learning model. This highlights how policy solutions that only limit third-party data sharing and selling would not be enough to prevent these predatory advertisements. Rather, policymakers must ban online behavioral ads.\nL110: What DraftKings is doing with their targeted promotions is just one example of how online behavioral advertising causes real harm to real people. But there are ways to take back control over your own data: EFF offers resources such as our cite56†Surveillance Self Defense project,†ssd.eff.org along with other tips for how you can protect yourself cite57†on mobile apps and cite58†on websites†privacybadger.org .\nL111: DraftKings’ use of AI to target losing gamblers illustrates how ad tech evolves and how companies find new ways to use our data against us. This is why EFF believes that cite48†all behavioral advertising should be banned . If companies can’t send personalized ads, they’ll have less incentive to collect the behavioral data powering them.\nL112: ## Related Issues\nL113: \nL114: cite59†Online Behavioral Tracking L115: \nL116: cite8†Privacy L117: \nL118: ## Related Updates\nL119: \nL120: cite60†Deeplinks Blog by cite61†Lena Cohen , cite62†Paige Collings | June 26, 2026\nL121: ### cite63†EFF to Grindr: This Pride Month, Put Safety and Privacy Over Profits L122: \nL123: This Pride month, we’re calling on the dating app Grindr to prioritize LGBTQ+ user safety by making privacy the default across its platform. That means no more sharing personal data with advertisers or training AI on private information without users’ opt-in consent.\nL124: \nL125: cite60†Deeplinks Blog by cite61†Lena Cohen , cite64†Hudson Hongo | March 5, 2026\nL126: ### cite54†The Government Uses Targeted Advertising to Track Your Location. Here's What We Need to Do. L127: \nL128: The online advertising industry has built a massive surveillance machine, and the government can co-opt it to spy on us.\nL129: \nL130: cite60†Deeplinks Blog by cite61†Lena Cohen | January 29, 2026\nL131: ### cite65†Google Settlement May Bring New Privacy Controls for Real-Time Bidding L132: \nL133: EFF has long warned about the dangers of the “real-time bidding” (RTB) system powering nearly every ad you see online. A proposed class-action settlement with Google over their RTB system is a step in the right direction towards giving people more control over their data. Truly curbing the harms of...\nL134: \nL135: cite60†Deeplinks Blog by cite61†Lena Cohen | September 4, 2025\nL136: ### cite66†From Libraries to Schools: Why Organizations Should Install Privacy Badger L137: \nL138: ​​In an era of pervasive online surveillance, organizations have an important role to play in protecting their communities’ privacy. Schools, libraries, and other organizations can make private browsing the norm by deploying Privacy Badger on their computers.\nL139: \nL140: cite60†Deeplinks Blog by cite61†Lena Cohen , cite67†Rory Mir | June 20, 2025\nL141: ### cite68†Protect Yourself From Meta’s Latest Attack on Privacy L142: \nL143: The best way to stop this cycle of invasive tracking techniques and patchwork fixes is to ban online behavioral advertising. This would end the practice of targeting ads based on your online activity, removing the primary incentive for companies to track and share your personal data. We need strong federal...\nL144: \nL145: cite60†Deeplinks Blog by cite61†Lena Cohen | March 27, 2025\nL146: ### cite69†Online Tracking is Out of Control—Privacy Badger Can Help You Fight Back L147: \nL148: Every time you browse the web, you're being tracked. That’s why EFF created Privacy Badger, a free, open source browser extension used by millions to fight corporate surveillance and take back control of their data.\nL149: \nL150: cite60†Deeplinks Blog by cite61†Lena Cohen | January 17, 2025\nL151: ### cite70†Mad at Meta? Don't Let Them Collect and Monetize Your Personal Data L152: \nL153: If you’re fed up with Meta right now, you’re not alone. Meta tracks you across millions of websites and apps and its business model relies on your data. If you want to limit Meta’s ability to collect and profit from your personal data, here’s what you need to know.\nL154: \nL155: cite60†Deeplinks Blog by cite71†David Greene | January 17, 2025\nL156: ### cite72†EFF Statement on U.S. Supreme Court's Decision to Uphold TikTok Ban L157: \nL158: Shutting down a communications platform or forcing its reorganization based on concerns of foreign propaganda and anti-national manipulation is an eminently anti-democratic tactic, one that the US has previously condemned globally.\nL159: \nL160: cite60†Deeplinks Blog by cite61†Lena Cohen | January 6, 2025\nL161: ### cite47†Online Behavioral Ads Fuel the Surveillance Industry—Here’s How L162: \nL163: Each time you see a targeted ad, your personal information is exposed to thousands of advertisers and data brokers through a process called “real-time bidding” (RTB). This process does more than deliver ads—it fuels government surveillance, poses national security risks, and gives data brokers easy access to your online activity...\nL164: \nL165: cite60†Deeplinks Blog by cite71†David Greene | December 18, 2024\nL166: ### cite73†EFF Statement on U.S. Supreme Court's Decision to Consider TikTok Ban L167: \nL168: The TikTok ban itself and the DC Circuit's approval of it should be of great concern even to those who find TikTok undesirable or scary. Shutting down communications platforms or forcing their reorganization based on concerns of foreign propaganda and anti-national manipulation is an eminently anti-democratic tactic, one that the...\nL169: \nL170: ## Related Issues\nL171: \nL172: cite59†Online Behavioral Tracking L173: \nL174: cite8†Privacy L175: \nL176: cite0†Back to top L177: ## Follow EFF:\nL178: \nL179:   * cite74†mastodon†mastodon.social L180:   * cite75†facebook†www.facebook.com L181:   * cite76†instagram†www.instagram.com L182:   * cite77†Blue Sky†bsky.app L183:   * cite78†youtube†www.youtube.com L184:   * cite79†flicker†www.flickr.com L185:   * cite80†linkedin†www.linkedin.com L186:   * cite81†tiktok†www.tiktok.com L187:   * cite82†threads†www.threads.net L188: \nL189: Check out our 4-star rating on cite83†Charity Navigator†www.charitynavigator.org .\nL190: \nL191: ## Contact\nL192: \nL193:   * cite2†General L194:   * cite84†Legal L195:   * cite85†Security L196:   * cite2†Membership L197:   * cite3†Press L198: ## About\nL199: \nL200:   * cite86†Calendar L201:   * cite23†Volunteer L202:   * cite87†Victories L203:   * cite88†History L204:   * cite89†Internships L205:   * cite90†Jobs†www.paycomonline.net L206:   * cite4†Staff L207:   * cite91†Diversity & Inclusion L208: \nL209: ## Issues\nL210: \nL211:   * cite7†Free Speech L212:   * cite8†Privacy L213:   * cite9†Creativity & Innovation L214:   * cite10†Transparency L215:   * cite11†International L21